<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  .github/integration.runsettings — VSTest config for the CI `integration` job ONLY.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  The docker capstones each stand up a REAL Aspire topology via
  DistributedApplication.StartAsync, which enforces DCP's internal ~20s
  per-resource startup watchdog. On a loaded CI runner, if MANY topologies
  start at once, none reaches "ready" inside that ~20s window — surfacing as
  an intermittent EnvironmentError on 'startup' ([HealthGate] timeout of
  '00:00:20'): a flake, not a defect.

  Two layers serialise topology startup:
    A) WITHIN each docker-bearing assembly — [assembly: CollectionBehavior(
       DisableTestParallelization = true)] in Runtime.Tests + Orchestration.Tests
       (mirrors the Compilation.Tests precedent).
    B) ACROSS assemblies (this file) — `dotnet test vouchfx.sln` otherwise runs
       Runtime.Tests + Orchestration.Tests (+ Compilation.Tests) as SEPARATE test
       hosts IN PARALLEL via VSTest, so step (A) alone is not enough.

  THE KNOB
  RunConfiguration/MaxCpuCount is the documented VSTest lever:
  "Degree of parallelization, spawns `n` test hosts to run tests. Default: 1.
   Max: Number of cpu cores." (microsoft/vstest docs/configure.md). Setting it
  to 1 forces VSTest to run ONE test host (one assembly) at a time, so at most
  one topology starts at any moment, comfortably inside DCP's ~20s window.
  `dotnet test` honours this file when passed via its `settings` option
  (docs/RunSettingsArguments.md).

  SCOPE
  CI-ONLY: applied solely via the `settings` option in the `integration` job's
  `dotnet test` step in build.yml. The fast `build` job does NOT use this file
  and keeps its normal cross-assembly parallelism.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <!-- 1 = serialise topology startup so at most one test assembly (one
         topology) runs at a time, fitting DCP's ~20s per-resource window on
         loaded runners. -->
    <MaxCpuCount>1</MaxCpuCount>
  </RunConfiguration>
</RunSettings>
